{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "```python\ndef is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a string is happy based on length and distinct consecutive letters.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(s) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(len(s) - 2):\n        # Check if any of the three consecutive characters are the same\n        if not (s[i] != s[i+1] and s[i] != s[i+2] and s[i+1] != s[i+2]):\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/82", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef prime_length(string):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the length of the input string is a prime number.\n    Returns True if the string length is prime, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    length = len(string)\n\n    if length <= 1:\n        return False\n    if length == 2:\n        return True\n    if length % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of length\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(length)) + 1, 2):\n        if length % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    # For n > 1, apply the principle of inclusion-exclusion: |A U B| = |A| + |B| - |A \u2229 B|\n    # A: n-digit numbers starting with 1.\n    #    The first digit is 1 (1 choice). The remaining (n-1) digits can be any from 0-9 (10 choices each).\n    #    Count_A = 1 * 10^(n-1)\n    #\n    # B: n-digit numbers ending with 1.\n    #    The last digit is 1 (1 choice). The first digit cannot be 0 (9 choices: 1-9).\n    #    The remaining (n-2) middle digits can be any from 0-9 (10 choices each).\n    #    Count_B = 9 * 10^(n-2)\n    #\n    # A \u2229 B: n-digit numbers starting AND ending with 1.\n    #    The first digit is 1 (1 choice). The last digit is 1 (1 choice).\n    #    The remaining (n-2) middle digits can be any from 0-9 (10 choices each).\n    #    Count_A_and_B = 1 * 10^(n-2)\n    #\n    # Total = Count_A + Count_B - Count_A_and_B\n    # Total = 10^(n-1) + 9 * 10^(n-2) - 10^(n-2)\n    # Total = 10 * 10^(n-2) + 9 * 10^(n-2) - 1 * 10^(n-2)\n    # Total = (10 + 9 - 1) * 10^(n-2)\n    # Total = 18 * 10^(n-2)\n\n    return 18 * (10**(n - 2))\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/84", "solution": "```python\ndef solve(N):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer N, return the total sum of its digits in binary.\"\"\"\n\n    # Calculate the sum of decimal digits of N\n    sum_of_digits = 0\n    # Convert N to a string to easily iterate over its digits\n    for digit_char in str(N):\n        sum_of_digits += int(digit_char)\n\n    # Convert the sum_of_digits to its binary string representation\n    decimal_num = sum_of_digits\n\n    if decimal_num == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n\n    binary_digits = []\n    temp_num = decimal_num\n    while temp_num > 0:\n        binary_digits.append(str(temp_num % 2))\n        temp_num //= 2\n\n    return \"\".join(reversed(binary_digits))\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/85", "solution": "```python\ndef add(lst):\n    \"\"\"Given a non-empty list of integers lst. add the even elements that are at odd indices..\n\n\n    Examples:\n        add([4, 2, 6, 7]) ==> 2\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    for i, element in enumerate(lst):\n        if not isinstance(element, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n        if i % 2 != 0 and element % 2 == 0:  # Odd index and even element\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function that takes a string and returns an ordered version of it.\n    Ordered version of string, is a string where all words (separated by space)\n    are replaced by a new word where all the characters arranged in\n    ascending order based on ascii value.\n    Note: You should keep the order of words and blank spaces in the sentence.\n    \"\"\"\n    import re\n\n    # Split the string while keeping delimiters (spaces)\n    # This regex splits by spaces but keeps them in the result list\n    parts = re.split(r'(\\s+)', s)\n\n    result_parts = []\n    for part in parts:\n        if part.strip() == '':  # If the part is entirely spaces or empty, append it as is.\n            result_parts.append(part)\n        else:  # Otherwise, it's a word; sort its characters and append.\n            result_parts.append(\"\".join(sorted(part)))\n\n    return \"\".join(result_parts)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/81", "solution": "def numerical_letter_grade(grades):\n    \"\"\"It is the last week of the semester and the teacher has to give the grades\n    to students. The teacher has been making her own algorithm for grading.\n    The only problem is, she has lost the code she used for grading.\n    She has given you a list of GPAs for some students and you have to write\n    a function that can output a list of letter grades using the following table:\n             GPA       |    Letter grade\n              4.0                A+\n            > 3.7                A\n            > 3.3                A-\n            > 3.0                B+\n            > 2.7                B\n            > 2.3                B-\n            > 2.0                C+\n            > 1.7                C\n            > 1.3                C-\n            > 1.0                D+\n            > 0.7                D\n            > 0.0                D-\n              0.0                E\n\n\n    Example:\n    grade_equation([4.0, 3, 1.7, 2, 3.5]) ==> ['A+', 'B', 'C-', 'C', 'A-']\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _get_single_letter_grade(gpa):\n        # Converts a single GPA to its corresponding letter grade based on the defined scale.\n        if gpa == 4.0:\n            return 'A+'\n        elif gpa > 3.7:\n            return 'A'\n        elif gpa > 3.3:\n            return 'A-'\n        elif gpa > 3.0:\n            return 'B+'\n        elif gpa > 2.7:\n            return 'B'\n        elif gpa > 2.3:\n            return 'B-'\n        elif gpa > 2.0:\n            return 'C+'\n        elif gpa > 1.7:\n            return 'C'\n        elif gpa > 1.3:\n            return 'C-'\n        elif gpa > 1.0:\n            return 'D+'\n        elif gpa > 0.7:\n            return 'D'\n        elif gpa > 0.0:\n            return 'D-'\n        elif gpa == 0.0:\n            return 'E'\n        else:\n            # For any GPA less than 0.0, it also defaults to 'E'.\n            return 'E'\n\n    letter_grades = []\n    for gpa in grades:\n        letter_grades.append(_get_single_letter_grade(gpa))\n    return letter_grades"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, return a copy of the given array after sorting,\n    you will sort the given array in ascending order if the sum( first index value, last index value) is odd,\n    or sort it in descending order if the sum( first index value, last index value) is even.\n\n    Note:\n    * don't change the given array.\n\n    Examples:\n    * sort_array([]) => []\n    * sort_array([5]) => [5]\n    * sort_array([2, 4, 3, 0, 1, 5]) => [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n    * sort_array([2, 4, 3, 0, 1, 5, 6]) => [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        return []\n    if len(array) == 1:\n        return array[:]\n\n    first_element = array[0]\n    last_element = array[-1]\n    sum_first_last = first_element + last_element\n\n    if sum_first_last % 2 != 0:\n        return sorted(array)\n    else:\n        return sorted(array, reverse=True)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "```python\ndef get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integers x in the 2D list and returns a list of their (row, column) coordinates.\n    Coordinates are sorted by row ascending, then by column descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    found_coordinates = []\n    for r_idx, row in enumerate(lst):\n        for c_idx, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val == x:\n                found_coordinates.append((r_idx, c_idx))\n\n    # Sort primarily by row (ascending), then by column (descending)\n    found_coordinates.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], -coord[1]))\n    return found_coordinates\n```"}
